  "summary": "Decorative wood technique inifying contrasting veneers to create pictorial designs",
  "description": "Involves inlaying different colored woods and materials to create images or patterns. Flourished during Renaissance as decorative art form for furniture and architectural elements.",
  "culturalContext": "Marquetry and intarsia — veneer-picture craft from Fra Giovanni's Siena choir stalls (intarsia, 15th c.) through André-Charles Boulle's brass-tortoiseshell marquetry (Louis XIV) to thistle-and-tulip Italian profilo work — turned flat wood into optics: perspective room illusions, floral trophies, landscape panoramas in 20+ species per panel. The Boulle \"première-partie\" and \"contre-partie\" trick (one cutting, two mirror images) is craft legend; the tradition feeds violin-making, luxury joinery and CNC-era revival.",
